    "acquired_at": "2026-11-24T20:29:39.706327+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-24T20:29:39.706327+00:00",
    "body": "EICMA 2026 • MTPeak Stole Some of the Spotlight at EICMA 2026 With Insane Power and Retro Looks on Three Wheels\nReggie Jackson's Ferrari 250 GT Lusso Failed To Clear Reserve After High Bid Hit $1.1 Million\n14 photos\nPhoto: P1Miami on Bring a Trailer\nSomeone put $1,103,019 on the table for a 1963 Ferrari 250 GT Lusso. Not any ol' GTL, but chassis number 250GTL5117, namely the car owned by Reggie Jackson between 1998 and 2008. Alas, the Bring a Trailer auction closed with the reserve unmet, and it's easy to understand why.\nEvery single GTL auctioned thus far in 2026 fetched over a mil, with chassis number 5593 changing hands for $3.3 million at Mecum Kissimmee in January. Given this information, the seller was perfectly in the right not to be pleased with merely $1.1 million for the ex-Reggie Jackson car.\nFerrari produced just around 350 examples between 1962 and 1964, and the Scaglietti-bodied Lusso is definitely a looker. One of the prettiest Fezzas of all time also marked the end of the 250 series. It and the race-oriented GTO were replaced by the punchier 275 and 330 lines for 1964.\nThis particular vehicle comes with Ferrari Classiche certification and an extensive ownership file. Still, it's not a perfect 250 GT Lusso. This one has changed paint colors, received a substantial refurbishment, accumulated some paint damage, and carries a replacement limited-slip differential instead of the original unit.\nThe 125th 250 GT Lusso built was completed on October 19, 1963, then subsequently delivered to a Rome-based dealer. A construction company acquired it before selling the car to a Swiss gentleman in May 1964. Six years later, Luigi Chinetti Motors brought the car to the United States of America.\nPhoto: P1Miami on Bring a Trailer Its Rhode Island-based owner from that era kept the V12-powered grand tourer for a quarter of a century. Baseball Hall of Famer Reggie Jackson entered the scene in 1998, commissioning Grand Prix Motors in California to refurbish the car two years later.\nWork included a paint job, switching from the original Marrone to Pino Verde. Tobacco leather upholstery also needs mentioning, another unoriginal element that affects 250GTL5117's value.\nJackson ultimately sold the Ferrari in 2008 to a Midwest collector, who entrusted further work to Greg Jones. The following year, chassis number 5117 collected a Platinum Award at the Cavallino Classic in Florida. Classiche certification followed in 2011, while the current owner got their hands on it back in 2018.\nPictured with cracked paint aft of the passenger door, 5117 rolls on Michelin XVS rubber mounted on 15-inch Borrani wire wheels. A matching spare sits in the trunk. Under the hood, you will find a 3.0-liter naturally aspirated V12 and three Weber carbs. All three were rebuilt in 2026, while the engine received a valve job during the Greg Jones refurbishment from two decades ago.\nThe oil, spark plugs, and fuel filter were changed in 2026 as well, yet the listing mentions a bit of seepage on certain mechanical components. Prior to the Bring a Trailer auction, the odometer showed 30,359 original kilometers (18,864 miles). The seller added just around 50 clicks (31 miles).\nOffered with a Marcel Massini report, historical invoices, an owner's manual, previous ownership photos, and a tool kit, the Montana-titled car has plenty going for it. The bidding result, however, is a reminder that provenance and a seven-figure reserve don't always find common ground.\nVia // Bring a Trailer\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nferrari 250 gt lusso Ferrari Reggie Jackson auction for sale\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1-of-1 1967 Pontiac Firebird \"Skydiver\" Show Car Parachutes Back Into the Spotlight After 60 Years",
